# Line endings are decided here, not by whichever editor saved last.
#
# There was no .gitattributes and core.autocrlf=false, which together mean the
# bytes a tool writes to disk are the bytes committed. That is how five files in
# the Pro repo came to be stored with CRLF among LF neighbours, and how a
# thirty-line change to routes.ts once landed as 2,492 changed lines with blame
# on every one of them pointing at the reformat instead of the author.
#
# text=auto lets Git decide what is text and normalise it to LF in the object
# store. eol=lf checks it back out as LF on every platform, and that half is the
# one doing the work here: this is developed on Windows, so without it a
# checkout would write CRLF and every working tree would disagree with the
# repository all over again.
#
# This replaces a convention with a rule. CLAUDE.md still describes the rule for
# anything editing files outside Git's normalisation.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, stated rather than left to detection. Auto-detection already gets
# these right; saying so means a heuristic that changes its mind can never
# rewrite an icon as text. PDFs are listed ahead of need — this app stores them,
# and a corrupted fixture would fail in a way nobody would think to look for.
*.png  binary
*.ico  binary
*.icns binary
*.pdf  binary
